data.content:
Here are the idiomatic MSTest replacements:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0);
    Assert.IsTrue(users.Any());                          // no built-in "IsNotEmpty"

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive));
    CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreNotNull(users);          // if nulls matter
    Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive));           // All() is fine here — no better built-in

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count() == 3);
    Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count);                     // expected first, actual second

    // Assert.IsTrue(admin != null);
    Assert.IsNotNull(users.Single(u => u.Role == "Admin"));
}
```

**Key MSTest assertions for collections:**

| Instead of | Use |
|---|---|
| `Assert.IsTrue(x != null)` | `Assert.IsNotNull(x)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(count == n)` | `Assert.AreEqual(n, count)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(a == b)` | `Assert.AreEqual(a, b)` |
| check list contents | `CollectionAssert.Contains(col, item)` |
| check two lists match | `CollectionAssert.AreEqual(expected, actual)` |
| check same items, any order | `CollectionAssert.AreEquivalent(expected, actual)` |
| all items match a type | `CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreInstancesOfType(col, type)` |
| no nulls | `CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreNotNull(col)` |
| all unique | `CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreUnique(col)` |

MSTest's `CollectionAssert` doesn't have a predicate-based `AllMatch` or `IsNotEmpty`, so `Assert.IsTrue(users.All(...))` and `Assert.IsTrue(users.Any())` are still the right calls for those cases. The main wins are `AreEqual`, `IsNotNull`, and the `CollectionAssert` helpers — they give much better failure messages than a bare `IsTrue(false)`.
